---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Circuit Mapping | Shabel Lab">
    <!-- Back Button -->
    <div class="navigation-aid">
        <a href="/research" class="back-link">&larr; Back to All Research</a>
    </div>

    <!-- Deep Dive Content -->
    <article class="research-detail">
        <h1>Lateral Habenula Circuit Mapping</h1>
        
        <div class="featured-image-placeholder">
            <p>High-resolution diagram goes here</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Project Overview</h2>
        <p>The lateral habenula (LHb) has emerged as a key structure in encoding negative valence and driving behavioral avoidance. Despite its importance, the exact topographic organization of its inputs from the basal ganglia and its outputs to the monoaminergic centers remains complex.</p>
        
        <p>In this project, our lab seeks to dissect these specific pathways using state-of-the-art methodology. We aim to determine how distinct populations of LHb neurons integrate specific sensory and cognitive information to compute aversive values.</p>

        <h2>Methodology</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Viral Tracing:</strong> Utilizing retrograde and anterograde AAV vectors to visualize monosynaptic connections.</li>
            <li><strong>In Vivo Electrophysiology:</strong> Recording neuronal firing patterns in awake, behaving subjects during reward and punishment tasks.</li>
            <li><strong>Optogenetics:</strong> Precisely stimulating or inhibiting specific LHb pathways to observe real-time behavioral shifts.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Current Objectives</h2>
        <p>We are currently focused on identifying the specific genetic markers of the LHb neurons that project directly to the Ventral Tegmental Area (VTA), aiming to understand how these cells modulate dopamine release during stress.</p>
    </article>
</Layout>

